{"name":"Grassy Glade","hex":"#d8ddca","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grassy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered with grass.","Resembling grass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of grassie (“grass carp; white amur”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grassy"},{"term":"glade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An open passage through a wood; a grassy open or cleared space in a forest.","An everglade."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glade (geography)","blocks":[{"text":"In the most general sense, a glade or clearing is an open area within a forest. Glades are often grassy meadows under the canopy of deciduous trees such as red alder or quaking aspen in western North America. They also represent openings in forests where local conditions such as avalanches, poor soils, or fire damage have created semipermanent clearings. They are very important to herbivorous animals, such as deer and elk, for forage and denning activities."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Cox_Valley2.JPG/330px-Cox_Valley2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glade_(geography)","wikidata":"Q4358873"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alderglade.JPG/500px-Alderglade.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alderglade.JPG/1280px-Alderglade.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alderglade.JPG/500px-Alderglade.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An alder glade along the Elwha River","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlderglade.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikim